# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  Buddy — Git line-ending normalisation
#
#  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS:
#  Without it, git on Windows (core.autocrlf=true) converts LF → CRLF on
#  checkout. Python on Windows can handle CRLF in .py files, BUT compiled
#  .pyc files are binary-matched to the exact source bytes. If Windows git
#  changes line endings after the IDE generated the .pyc, the checksum
#  mismatches and Python falls back to recompiling — which is fine, BUT
#  if the .pyc itself was committed (as happened here), Windows would get
#  a Linux-generated .pyc that doesn't match the CRLF source → import errors.
#
#  SOLUTION:
#  1. Always store files in the repo with LF (eol=lf).
#  2. Never commit .pyc / __pycache__ (enforced by .gitignore).
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Default: normalise all text files to LF in the repo
* text=auto eol=lf

# Python source — always LF
*.py    text eol=lf

# Web / config files — always LF
*.js    text eol=lf
*.jsx   text eol=lf
*.ts    text eol=lf
*.tsx   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.html  text eol=lf
*.css   text eol=lf
*.env   text eol=lf
*.env.* text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.toml  text eol=lf
*.cfg   text eol=lf
*.ini   text eol=lf

# Binary — never touch line endings
*.pyc   binary
*.pyd   binary
*.pyo   binary
*.pkl   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.gz    binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.eot   binary
